course: politics
lesson: 14
title: Dad shouting at the TV: big feelings about politics
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Politics can feel like a football match to grown-ups, because the decisions touch their jobs, their bills and their families. So sometimes Dad shouts at the TV the way fans shout at a referee who cannot hear them.
explain: That shout is a way of letting out frustration, called venting. It does not change the news, and it is never about you.
explain: The news shows what went wrong today because it is new and important; it does not show the millions of people having a normal day. A worry about "the country" is not the same as danger in your own house.
explain: When news makes you scared or sad, the best tools are small ones: say the worry out loud to a grown-up you trust, ask them to explain, take a news break, and do one small kind thing. Fixing wars is not a child's job.
explain: Grown-ups say it in their own words: a Welsh mum might be "tamping" (furious) at the news and then give you a "cwtch" (a cuddle), a Northern gran might call a scared child "nesh" (feels the cold, a bit soft), and an American dad might say "don't have a cow" (calm down).
fact: Politics feels personal to grown-ups because it touches their jobs, money and family.
fact: Shouting at the TV is venting, letting out frustration; it changes nothing, and it is not about the child.
fact: Grown-ups feel sad when they see people suffering, even far away; this is empathy, and it is all right to ask what made them sad.
fact: News about prices rising for the whole country, called inflation or the cost of living, does not mean a family is in danger; a grown-up at home knows what is really happening.
fact: News shows the worst things happening because they are new; taking a break from the news is all right.
fact: Some say children should be kept away from grown-up news; others say gentle kids' news shows, like Australia's Behind the News, are better than half-heard rumours.
fact: When people feel strongly they often exaggerate; countries go on after elections, and power passing peacefully from one side to the other is a normal part of democracy.
fact: Politics feels most personal when a decision touches your own job, like nurses' pay; some nurses have gone on strike to ask for more pay.
fact: Big problems like wars are not a child's responsibility; kids can help in small ways, like being kind, learning or giving to a charity with a grown-up.
fact: Asking a grown-up to explain the news makes it less confusing; grown-ups' feelings usually come from their values, like fairness or safety.
fact: Too much news can cause news fatigue; some families make news-free times, like dinner.
fact: Some talk radio and TV shows stir up outrage on purpose, because angry listeners keep listening.
fact: People disagree whether staying informed every day is a duty or whether a short check-in each day is enough.
exercise: Why can politics feel personal to grown-ups? | It touches their jobs, money and family | It is only a game that is shown on the TV | They know every politician in person, by name | They have to go out and vote in an election every day
exercise: What is venting? | Letting out strong feelings | Fixing a problem | Voting in an election | Changing the TV channel
exercise: When Dad shouts at the news, whose fault is it? | Nobody's; it is not about the child | The child's | The TV's | The neighbour's
exercise: What is empathy? | Feeling what someone else is feeling | Shouting at a screen | Not caring about the news | Agreeing with everyone
exercise: What does inflation mean? | Prices keep going up over time | Balloons get bigger | Pay goes up every day | Shops close at night
exercise: The radio says prices are rising across the country. Does that mean your family is in danger? | Not necessarily; ask a grown-up what is happening at home | Yes, every family in the country will soon lose its house and its car | Yes, the radio always means you | No, prices never matter
exercise: Why does the news show so many bad things? | They are new and important, so they make the news | Nothing good ever happens anywhere in the world, so there is nothing else to show | Reporters only like bad things | Good news is against the rules
exercise: What does exaggeration mean? | Making something sound bigger or worse than it is | Telling a story exactly | Saying nothing at all | Asking a question
exercise: What is a peaceful transfer of power? | When the loser hands over to the winner without fighting | When the winner refuses to start the job and the loser stays on instead | When nobody votes | When the army picks the leader
exercise: What is a strike? | When workers stop working to push for better pay or conditions | When a leader is punched | When a ball hits a bat hard in a game of cricket or baseball, far into the crowd | When a shop has a sale
exercise: Whose job is it to stop a war? | Grown-ups, governments and many countries | A child with a good plan and a lot of courage | The TV channel that shows the war on the news | Nobody at all, because wars stop on their own
exercise: What small things can kids do about big problems? | Be kind, learn, or give to a charity with a grown-up | Fix the war alone by writing a letter to the leaders and waiting for an answer | Stay awake all night worrying | Stop going to school
exercise: What is news fatigue? | Feeling worn out from too much news | Being a news reporter | Missing the evening news | Liking the news too much
exercise: Why do some families have no news on at dinner? | So they can rest and talk about their day | Because news is against the law at the dinner table | Because the TV is broken and nobody can fix it | Because children must not know anything about the world
exercise: Why do some talk shows stay angry? | Angry listeners keep listening | Anger is needed to read the news | Hosts are always cross with everybody | Calm shows are banned from the radio
exercise: What helps a worry get smaller? | Saying it out loud to someone you trust | Keeping it secret | Reading more scary headlines | Shouting at the TV
test: Kai jumps when Dad yells "That's rubbish!" Dad says: "Sorry, mate, not you." What should Kai understand? | Dad is venting at the news, not cross with Kai | Kai did something wrong and Dad is too polite to say it | Dad wants Kai to shout at the TV with him | The TV heard Dad and will change the news
test: "Mum's tamping at the news again" says a Cardiff boy (tamping = furious). Then she gives him a cwtch (a cuddle). What is true? | Grown-ups can be angry at the news and still loving at home | She is angry with her son and is only pretending to be kind so he won't notice | Cuddles mean the news was good | Tamping means she is happy
test: «can't sleep, saw scary headlines on mum's phone, is the world ending fr» (fr = for real). What is the best answer? | No; the news shows what went wrong today, not everything in the world | Yes, headlines are always about the end of the world, so you should stay awake and keep watch | Read more headlines until you sleep | Never tell your mum
test: Uncle says "This country is finished!" after an election. What is the best reply? | Big feelings use big words; there will be another election in a few years | He must be right, countries always end after an election that your side has lost | Elections stop forever after one result | Only uncles can tell
test: Hannah writes a ten-step plan to stop a war and feels terrible when nothing happens. What would you tell her? | It is not her job to fix the world, but small kindness counts | Write a longer plan with twenty steps and send it to every leader in the world | Stop caring about anyone | Plans by children always work
test: Fatima's dad, a nurse, gets upset when the news talks about nurses' pay. Why? | The decision touches his own job | He hates the news reader on that channel | Nurses are not allowed to watch TV at all | He wants to be a politician one day soon
test: "Don't have a cow, kiddo, it's just politics" says Grandpa from Ohio (don't have a cow = calm down). The child asks why Mum is angry. What is a good next step? | Ask Mum to explain in simple words | Stay quiet forever | Shout back at the TV | Guess and not ask
test: A radio host is yelling in the car. Dad says, "Right, that's enough," and switches to music. What would happen if they kept listening for hours? | They might just get more upset without learning more | They would learn every fact calmly, because shouting helps people remember things | The host would calm down | The news would change
test: "Nah, I'm not nesh, I can watch the war news" says a lad from Sheffield (nesh = a bit soft). Afterwards he feels awful. What is OK? | Taking a news break and talking to a grown-up | Watching even more war news to toughen himself up | Never telling anyone how awful he feels about it | Pretending to be fine forever, because boys don't cry
test: «my family's got a no news at dinner rule, are we hiding from the world??» What is true? | No; turning the news off for a while is resting, not running away | Yes, it is against the law to switch off the news when something big is happening | Yes, they will never know anything | No, because news is always fake
test: Mum wipes her eyes at a report about a far-away war. Is it OK to ask her why? | Yes, feeling sad for others is normal and asking is fine | No, it is rude to ask a grown-up why they are sad, so you should leave the room | No, she is sad because of you | Yes, but only after a week
exam: Identify the principal reason for which political matters may elicit strong emotional responses in adults. | Such matters bear directly upon their employment, finances and families | Adults are obliged by law to express their emotions concerning every decision of the government | Such matters are wholly unrelated to daily life | Adults are informed of all decisions before they are made
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the content of news reports? | They emphasise events that are new and significant, frequently those that have gone wrong | They present a complete account of every event occurring in the world, both favourable and unfavourable | They report exclusively favourable events | They report only events concerning the viewer's family
exam: Select the response consistent with the established guidance for a child distressed by news concerning a distant war. | The child may confide in a trusted adult, take a break from the news and perform small acts of kindness | The child shall be held responsible for ending the war | The child shall consume additional news, including footage of the conflict, until the distress subsides of its own accord | The child shall conceal the distress from all persons
exam: Determine the meaning of the expression "peaceful transfer of power" as employed in respect of elections. | The relinquishment of office by the losing side to the winning side without violence | The retention of office by the losing side | The appointment of a leader by the armed forces, followed by the transfer of the losing side's offices to the army | The abolition of all future elections
